course: dictionary
lesson: 6422
title: Mutter: speaking under your breath
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Mutter» means to speak quietly and unclearly, so other people cannot hear well. Often the speaker is talking to themselves. «It's giving zero interest,» Bertie mutters. The quiet, unclear voice tells you he is not really talking to anyone.
explain: Mutter can also mean complaining quietly, usually in a grumpy way. «The boys look at her, look at each other, and drift off muttering.» Muttering here shows they are not happy, and they do not want to be heard.
explain: In a story, «muttered» is a speech verb, a fancier way to say «said». It tells the reader how the character feels. «Said» is often the best choice, because readers hardly notice it. Use «muttered» now and then to show a character who is grumpy, shy or secretive.
fact: «Mutter» means to speak quietly and unclearly, often to yourself.
fact: «Muttering about» someone or something means complaining quietly.
fact: Phrases you may meet: mutter to yourself, mutter under your breath, mutter about.
fact: As a noun, «a mutter» is quiet, unclear speech, such as «a mutter of complaint».
fact: In London slang, Harrison says «That's peak» and mutters it, meaning «so unfair, so bad».
exercise: «It's giving zero interest,» Bertie says in a quiet, unclear voice. What is he doing? | Reading aloud to the class | Shouting across the playground | *Speaking quietly and unclearly | Singing a song loudly
exercise: «The boys drift off muttering.» What does muttering suggest? | *Quiet grumbling, not wanting to be heard | Asking for help politely | Shouting with joy | Sleeping soundly
exercise: What does «mutter under your breath» mean? | Speak loudly to the whole class | Hold your breath while you count | *Say something quietly so others cannot hear | Breathe very deeply
test: Why does the author write «muttered» instead of «said»? | To show the character is very tall | Because said is a wrong word | *To show the character is grumpy, shy or secretive | To make the sentence longer
test: Harrison says, «That's peak,» and mutters it. What does «peak» mean here? | *So unfair, so bad | A new video game | The top of a mountain | Very happy and excited
exam: Which phrase uses «mutter» as a noun? | he mutters to himself | *a mutter of complaint | she muttered yesterday | they are muttering now
